[{:db/id 17592186124466 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-538fdcf0-8dcd-525c-becd-0317742dbf1a" "question-0a3b6388-98bd-5083-8d1c-f60a05890b23"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124557 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-5744243e-c58b-5537-916f-bee0cb473253"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-538fdcf0-8dcd-525c-becd-0317742dbf1a" :question/id #uuid "538fdcf0-8dcd-525c-becd-0317742dbf1a" :question/math-academy-id "q-72010" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true about the function $f(x,y)={e}^{\\sqrt{xy}}?$\n\n- Both partial derivatives exist at the point $(1,-1)$\n- ${f}_{y}(-1,-1)$ is defined\n- ${f}_{x}(0,1)$ is defined" :question/worked-solution "First, we compute the partial derivatives of $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & ={e}^{\\sqrt{xy}}\\cdot \\frac{y}{2\\sqrt{xy}} \\\\  & =\\frac{y{e}^{\\sqrt{xy}}}{2\\sqrt{xy}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & ={e}^{\\sqrt{xy}}\\cdot \\frac{x}{2\\sqrt{xy}} \\\\  & =\\frac{x{e}^{\\sqrt{xy}}}{2\\sqrt{xy}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine the statements one-by-one. \n\n- Statement I is false. If we substitute $(1,-1)$ into $f$, we get\n\n$$\nf(1,-1)={e}^{\\sqrt{(1)(-1)}}=-{e}^{\\sqrt{-1}}\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined since we have a negative number under the radical.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, the function $f$ is defined at $(-1,-1)$, where we have \n\n$$\nf(-1,-1)={e}^{\\sqrt{(-1)(-1)}}={e}^{\\sqrt{1}}={e}^{1}=e,\n$$\n\nand ${f}_{y}$ is also defined at $(-1,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(-1,-1) & =\\frac{(-1){e}^{\\sqrt{(-1)(-1)}}}{2\\sqrt{(-1)(-1)}}=-\\frac{{e}^{\\sqrt{1}}}{2\\sqrt{1}}=-\\frac{e}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. If we substitute $(0,1)$ into ${f}_{x}$, we get\n\n$$\n{f}_{x}(0,1)=1\\cdot \\frac{{e}^{\\sqrt{0\\cdot 1}}}{2\\sqrt{0\\cdot 1}}=\\frac{{e}^{0}}{2\\sqrt{0}}=\\frac{1}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined since we have division by zero.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'II  only'." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-90260639-fefe-562c-8509-7dc58803f6c2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-90260639-fefe-562c-8509-7dc58803f6c2" :answer-field/id #uuid "90260639-fefe-562c-8509-7dc58803f6c2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-2dbd6ccf-ed91-536c-bc41-cccc05325c4d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-5c5e8d7a-13cb-5c4a-af4c-8873999183d1" "answer-2ff6ca25-dc52-5f06-b4e5-fccf5f8c424e" "answer-feeb48aa-dc46-5157-aa75-f72619f1adb4" "answer-2dbd6ccf-ed91-536c-bc41-cccc05325c4d" "answer-98963520-6ef4-5229-b4a2-12278d12f050"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5c5e8d7a-13cb-5c4a-af4c-8873999183d1" :answer/id #uuid "5c5e8d7a-13cb-5c4a-af4c-8873999183d1" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2ff6ca25-dc52-5f06-b4e5-fccf5f8c424e" :answer/id #uuid "2ff6ca25-dc52-5f06-b4e5-fccf5f8c424e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-feeb48aa-dc46-5157-aa75-f72619f1adb4" :answer/id #uuid "feeb48aa-dc46-5157-aa75-f72619f1adb4" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2dbd6ccf-ed91-536c-bc41-cccc05325c4d" :answer/id #uuid "2dbd6ccf-ed91-536c-bc41-cccc05325c4d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-98963520-6ef4-5229-b4a2-12278d12f050" :answer/id #uuid "98963520-6ef4-5229-b4a2-12278d12f050" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-5744243e-c58b-5537-916f-bee0cb473253" :question/id #uuid "5744243e-c58b-5537-916f-bee0cb473253" :question/math-academy-id "q-132654" :question/problem "Given that $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xyz)$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\frac{1}{x},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{1}{y},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=\\frac{1}{z},\n$$\n\nfind the set of values $(x,y,z)$ on which all partial derivatives of $f$ exist." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xyz)$ is defined only when $xyz>0.$ So, the domain of $f$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{f}={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xyz>0}.\n$$\n\nAs for the partial derivatives, we have the following.\n\n- The function $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\frac{1}{x}$ is defined only when $x\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of ${f}_{x}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{x}}={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x\\ne 0}.\n$$\n\n- The function $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{1}{y}$ is defined only when $y\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of ${f}_{y}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{y}}={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0}.\n$$\n\n- The function $\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=\\frac{1}{z}$ is defined only when $z\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of ${f}_{z}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{z}}={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,z\\ne 0}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partial derivatives of $f$ exist on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{x}}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{y}}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{z}} & ={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xyz>0}\\cap {(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x\\ne 0}\\cap {(x,y,z)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0}\\cap {(x,y,z)\\,:\\,z\\ne 0} \\\\  & ={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xyz>0}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-c3c16b06-37a6-5eac-b3ba-318617c8302e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-c3c16b06-37a6-5eac-b3ba-318617c8302e" :answer-field/id #uuid "c3c16b06-37a6-5eac-b3ba-318617c8302e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-094c985d-13f5-5f69-aeaa-14eacd51632b" :answer-field/choices ["answer-1b8221d1-3113-502e-8743-5e1721ad92fc" "answer-65397964-92f2-5396-b4dd-4062f6c76e9f" "answer-fe8bb828-cf39-59be-bb0e-11477fd6ff9c" "answer-094c985d-13f5-5f69-aeaa-14eacd51632b" "answer-83b42671-0c9a-50d8-9e14-5be3dd21c197"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1b8221d1-3113-502e-8743-5e1721ad92fc" :answer/id #uuid "1b8221d1-3113-502e-8743-5e1721ad92fc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x+y+z>0}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-65397964-92f2-5396-b4dd-4062f6c76e9f" :answer/id #uuid "65397964-92f2-5396-b4dd-4062f6c76e9f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x\\ne 0,\\,y\\ne 0,\\,z\\ne 0}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fe8bb828-cf39-59be-bb0e-11477fd6ff9c" :answer/id #uuid "fe8bb828-cf39-59be-bb0e-11477fd6ff9c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xyz>0,\\,x+y+z\\ne 0}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-094c985d-13f5-5f69-aeaa-14eacd51632b" :answer/id #uuid "094c985d-13f5-5f69-aeaa-14eacd51632b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xyz>0}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-83b42671-0c9a-50d8-9e14-5be3dd21c197" :answer/id #uuid "83b42671-0c9a-50d8-9e14-5be3dd21c197" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xyz\\ge 0}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-0a3b6388-98bd-5083-8d1c-f60a05890b23" :question/id #uuid "0a3b6388-98bd-5083-8d1c-f60a05890b23" :question/math-academy-id "q-132726" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true about the function $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{y})?$\n\n- Both partial derivatives exist at the point $(\\sqrt{3},1)$\n- ${f}_{x}(1,\\sqrt{3})$ is defined\n- ${f}_{y}(0,1)$ is defined" :question/worked-solution "First, we compute the partial derivatives of $f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{(\\frac{x}{y})}^{2}+1}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{x}{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}}+1}\\cdot \\frac{1}{y} \\\\  & =\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{y} \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{(\\frac{x}{y})}^{2}+1}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{x}{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}}+1}\\cdot (-\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\cdot (-\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine the statements one-by-one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the function $f$ is defined at $(\\sqrt{3},1)$, where we have \n\n$$\nf(\\sqrt{3},1)=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{1})=\\frac{\\pi }{3},\n$$\n\nand both partial derivatives are also defined at $(\\sqrt{3},1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(\\sqrt{3},1) & =\\frac{1}{(\\sqrt{3})^{2}+{1}^{2}}=\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {f}_{y}(\\sqrt{3},1) & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{(\\sqrt{3})^{2}+{1}^{2}}=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, the function $f$ itself is defined at $(1,\\sqrt{3})$, where we have \n\n$$\nf(1,\\sqrt{3})=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}})=\\frac{\\pi }{6},\n$$\n\nand ${f}_{x}$ is also defined at $(1,\\sqrt{3}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(1,\\sqrt{3})=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{{1}^{2}+(\\sqrt{3})^{2}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is also true. Indeed, the function $f$ itself is defined at $(0,1)$, where we have \n\n$$\nf(0,1)=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{0}{1})=0,\n$$\n\nand ${f}_{y}$ is also defined at $(0,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(0,1)=-\\frac{0}{{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-e7c2354d-85e6-577f-a722-6c718f129147"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-e7c2354d-85e6-577f-a722-6c718f129147" :answer-field/id #uuid "e7c2354d-85e6-577f-a722-6c718f129147" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-d4667459-8749-5c76-8a2a-d3b084c75e44" :answer-field/choices ["answer-4257b911-5988-5866-b9ff-6170b641f678" "answer-c44c870f-1d5b-5d9f-93d5-da63006f4e75" "answer-d4667459-8749-5c76-8a2a-d3b084c75e44" "answer-8e6aa6de-7d86-5660-b9a6-849